SECTION 1: Introduction and Project Goal
1.1 What Problem Does This App Solve?
Computers and phones become full of lots of images, PDFs of homework, audio files and random downloads in various folders. Sometimes you have several versions of the same file, saved with different names (e.g. DSC_005.jpg and DSC_005_copy.jpg). This causes wastage of gigabytes of storage.
1.2 Why I Built It This Way
Project Aim: A digital photo and file management application that scans through a folder and sorts them into six categories. It can find 100% duplicate files and provides charts for how much space is being used. It will sort them very neatly into folders.
Safety: Designed to ensure you never accidentally lose any data. This is done by using Dry-Run Previews, Undo and Renaming of filename collisions.
SECTION 2: How the System Works (Overall Flowchart)
2.1 Step-by-Step Program Flow
The app can be broken down into these core modules and simple steps:

1) Choose folder & GUI (`main.py`): The user chooses a folder using `customtkinter` (with `tkinter.ttk.Treeview` for tables) across 6 tabs: Overview, All Files, Duplicates, Charts, Organize, and Export.

2) Scan all files (`scanner.py`): Scans the chosen directory and subfolders, using the `CATEGORIES` dictionary to classify files into 6 groups and summarize totals with `summarize_files()`.

3) Photo Details & Twin Detection (`image_metadata.py` & `duplicate.py`): `read_image_metadata()` extracts rich EXIF camera info, while `duplicate.py` compares file sizes and SHA-256 fingerprints to group identical twins.

4) Charts, History & Safe Action (`charts.py`, `database.py`, `organizer.py`, `exporter.py`): `charts.py` renders visual storage charts, `database.py` saves scan history in SQLite (`organizer_data.db`), `organizer.py` safely moves, copies, or quarantines files with Undo support, and `exporter.py` saves reports.


