SECTION 3: Breakdown of Main Code Files and Variables
3.1 main.py (The App Window and Dashboard)
The `main.py` file is responsible for providing the application with a brain and GUI. The `main.py` file uses `customtkinter` (along with `tkinter.ttk.Treeview` for tabular data lists) to create interactive controls and 6 main tabs (Overview, All Files, Duplicates, Charts, Organize, and Export). This is managed by the `OrganizerApp` class.

Linking All Files: Once a folder has been selected by the user, main.py will call on the scanning, duplicate finding and image analysis functions. The data from all 3 will be stored in a single dictionary (self.scanned_files, self.summary_data, and self.duplicate_data).

Smooth UI: As scanning thousands of files can take some time, main.py will run this in the background using threading.Thread. This ensures the application window and progress bar run smoothly.


3.2 scanner.py (Finding and Grouping Every File)
The scanner.py file is used to explore the selected directory. It will search through all subdirectories and get information on files.


Scan Directory: The scan_directory function uses os.walk to automatically scan through the main directory and subdirectories. This is done without scanning any hidden system files.


Classify Files Into 6 Categories: The classify_file function uses the CATEGORIES dictionary to check the file extension (.jpg, .pdf, .mp3 etc.) and assign the category as being Images, Documents, Audio, Video, Archives or Other.

Summary: All file names, paths, sizes (in bytes) and dates are stored in a list called files. The total number of files and sizes are calculated for each category using the summarize_files() function.


3.3 duplicate.py (Two-Step Twin Detection Using Size and SHA256)
The `duplicate.py` file acts as the detective of the application. It identifies any perfect duplicates of files that may have different names or be stored in different subfolders.

The find_duplicates function will look at each file and group them together based on their size_bytes. This will create groups of files with the same exact byte size. Files that are not the same size will be skipped to save time.

The compute_sha256 function will then look at each file in the same size group and generate a unique digital fingerprint for each file. This will look at each file in 64KB chunks.

If there are two or more files with the same fingerprint they will be stored in the duplicate_groups list. The first file to be kept will be marked and then the wasted_bytes will add up the sizes of the extra files so the user knows how much storage space they can save.


3.4 image_metadata.py (Reading Photo Details and Camera Info)
The `image_metadata.py` is used to analyse photos. When an image file is found by the scanner, it is processed using the Pillow (PIL) library in Python to extract details.
Extracting Image & Camera Information: The `read_image_metadata()` function extracts width, height, resolution, and format. It also extracts rich EXIF metadata including camera make, camera model, lens model, capture date, ISO speed, aperture, shutter speed, focal length, and GPS presence.


Extracting Camera Information (EXIF): Many images contain extra digital information called EXIF. This is used to extract the camera brand (camera_make), camera model (camera_model) and date the image was taken (date_taken).


Fallback: If there is no camera information on the downloaded image or the Pillow library is not installed on the computer the function will return N/A to prevent the app crashing.


3.5 organizer.py (Dry Run Preview, Safe Moving, and Undo Feature)
This file acts as the 'Safe Mover' of the project. It supports both Copy and Move modes, organizing files into neat category folders or by photo EXIF date (`Photos/YYYY-MM`), with options to create compressed backups (`create_zip_backup()`) or isolate duplicate copies in a safe `Duplicate_Quarantine/` folder (`quarantine_duplicate_file()`).
The first step in this process is to create a dry run preview using `build_organize_plan()`. This shows a clear preview plan mapping original file paths to their target destination paths before any file is touched.


When the user confirms, `execute_organize_plan()` performs the actual file operations. During this execution, the `get_unique_path()` helper automatically resolves name collisions by appending numbers like '_1' or '_2' to prevent overwriting existing files.


Finally, all file actions are logged into `_LAST_BATCH_HISTORY`. Clicking the undo feature triggers `undo_last_organize()`, which iterates through the batch history in reverse to restore every file safely back to its original location.


3.6 exporter.py (Saving Reports to CSV and JSON)
This file acts as a "Report Printer" which will allow the user to export all the scan results to their computer. They can then open it up in Excel, Google Sheets or another program.


1) Export the full file list (`export_to_csv()`): Takes all scanned file records and saves their name, path, category, size, date, and metadata into a clean CSV spreadsheet.


2) Export duplicates report (`export_duplicates_to_csv()`): Generates a targeted spreadsheet listing only duplicate file groups, their SHA-256 hashes, and total wasted space.


3) Export full JSON backup (`export_to_json()`): Exports a complete structured JSON file containing summary statistics, category counts, duplicate groups, and file metadata for easy backup or programmatic reuse.


3.7 generate_sample_data.py (Creating Test Files and Folders)
This file acts as a "Practice Sandbox Builder", generating a folder with sample files that can be used to test the application without causing any issues to your personal files or images.


Step 1: Creating the Practice Folder The function create_sample_dataset generates a new folder called test_data, with realistic sub-folders such as Documents, Photos, Music and Backups.


Step 2: Generating a variety of files The folders are populated with realistic sample files including coloured .jpg images (using Pillow), text documents (.txt), spreadsheets (.csv), audio files (.wav) and zip files (.zip).


